      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**—meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, on Earth, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the fundamental elixir of life, a unique physical substance, and the most abundant molecule on the planet's surface. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Scientific & Physical Properties\nWater is famous among scientists because it defies many normal rules of physics and chemistry. \n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (vapor/steam).\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it carries vital minerals and nutrients through our bodies and through ecosystems.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns into ice. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying all aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps human bodies from overheating.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\n* **Global Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth’s surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas.\n* **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Despite how wet the planet looks, **96.5% of Earth's water is saltwater** in the oceans. Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and soil.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly moving in a closed loop known as the water cycle (or hydrological cycle). It evaporates from the oceans into the air, condenses into clouds, falls back to Earth as rain or snow, and flows through rivers back to the sea. \n\n"
